<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Estonian Soviet Socialist Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1940 CE – 1991 CE · capital Tallinn</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Soviet Estonia, imposed by annexation in 1940 and ended by the Singing Revolution and the restoration of independence in 1991.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> occupied the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-estonia">Republic of Estonia</a> in June 1940 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molotov-ribbentrop-pact">Molotov-Ribbentrop Pact</a>. After rigged elections, a puppet parliament asked to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> was annexed in August 1940. German occupation followed in 1941-1944, and Soviet rule returned in 1944.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Soviet rule brought nationalisation, collectivisation and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">mass deportations</a>, especially in June 1941 and March 1949, when over 20,000 people were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>. Large-scale Russian-speaking immigration changed the population, and oil shale industry developed in the north-east. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-kabin">Johannes Käbin</a> led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-the-soviet-union">Communist Party</a> from 1950 to 1978. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia's</a> access to Finnish television made it unusually open to Western influence.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the late 1980s the Singing Revolution mobilised mass protest. The Supreme Soviet declared Estonian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> in 1988, and during the failed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> coup <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-estonia">Estonia</a> declared full independence on 20 August 1991, restoring the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/estonian-soviet-socialist-republic.jpg" alt="Estonian Soviet Socialist Republic" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: First-uploaded SVG file versions authored by Urmas, Nokka · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Estonian_Soviet_Socialist_Republic_(1953%E2%80%931990).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1940 CE – 1991 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tallinn</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Johannes Käbin, Arnold Rüütel</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheran Christianity (state atheism)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Estonian, Russian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/estonia">Estonia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Estonian SSR, Soviet Estonia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1966&amp;focus=estonian-soviet-socialist-republic" aria-label="Estonian Soviet Socialist Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5201 181 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Estonian Soviet Socialist Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5521 437L5521 437M5521 437L5820 533"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5521 437h0M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5521 437h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5521 437h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tallinn. <a href="/atlas?year=1966&amp;focus=estonian-soviet-socialist-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
